    {
      "executionState" : "enabled",
      "id" : "note",
      "kind" : "edit.markdownNote",
      "parameters" : {
        "text" : "# Tinderbox Valley · Generated textures\nAn aerial firefighting game seen straight down on a forested valley. A wildfire spreads across a 480 × 300 cell map as a cellular automaton that runs one GPU thread per cell every frame: flames heat their neighbours, run faster downwind and uphill, burn quickly through dry meadow and slowly through forest, throw embers ahead of strong fronts and leave charred ground behind; smoke drifts with the wind. Lakes and granite outcrops are natural firebreaks. On each Run, six fixed-seed FLUX.2 Klein 4B Distilled jobs paint 1024 × 1024 images of the forest canopy, the dry meadow, the granite, the lake surface and the burnt ground, plus the title poster. Asset Cache stores the six images as named slots and previews each one as it becomes ready; the game starts after all six are ready, and later frames reuse the bundle without running the model again. The cache keeps its images between Runs of the same workflow revision, so only the first Run (or a Run after editing the workflow or pressing Clear) generates them. The model package remains link-only; link the Klein nodes to the same 4B Distilled package as the bundled Klein example.\n\n**Controls.** Drag the Trackpad crosshair, or drag on the full-screen preview, to steer your water bomber: it flies towards the crosshair (shown on the map) and circles it. Fly across a lake to scoop water: the plane skims the surface by itself, and the WATER gauge fills while SCOOPING shows (a full tank takes about a second of skimming). Press Action or Space to drop the tank; the dashed outline ahead of the plane shows where the water will land. Action or Space also starts the game, starts each mission and continues from a result screen.\n\n**Goal.** Hold the fire back from the villages until the rain arrives (the clock at the top counts real seconds). Water puts out flames and soaks the ground, and wet ground stops the fire for a while, so you can either bomb the head of the fire or lay wet lines between the fire and a village. Watch the wind arrow: the fire runs downwind. Each mission says how many villages must survive; a village burns when the fire takes hold of its houses (its ring and icon flash orange when the fire is close). Putting out every flame before the rain wins early with a containment bonus. Three missions: Pine Hollow (one escaped campfire), Dry Lightning (lightning keeps starting new fires) and Red Flag Warning (a stronger wind that slowly veers, more lightning and four villages). Scores count saved villages, saved wildland and the containment bonus. If too many villages burn, press Space to retry the mission.\n\n**Settings.** The Game settings JSON sets `fireSpread` (0.4–2; lower makes the fire slower), `rainSeconds` (45–400, default 150), `firstMission` (1–3) and `planeSpeed` (0.6–1.5). Changing a setting restarts the run."
      },
      "resources" : {

      },
      "title" : "How to play"
    },
